  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Update: Have lowered rainfall totals, as now\nexpected bulk of heavier convective elements remain mostly\noffshore with much of ENC remaining in stable sector. Have thus\nlowered thunder chances quite a bit, removing mention of thunder\nfor most mainland zones today, with widespread overrunning\nprecip and cloud cover limiting destabilization. Coastal areas\nmay still pick up upwards of 3-5\", esp Downeast and ctrl/srn OBX\nnearest the warmer Gulf waters through the overnight. \n\nA plume of tropical moisture characterized by 2\"+ PWATs and \ndewpoints in the 70s is forecast to get pulled north through the\nsoutheastern CONUS over the weekend to the east of the \nnorthward-advancing remnants of TC Isaias. At the surface, a \nwarm front/coastal trough will trudge north along the \nsoutheastern coastline, likely with a weak, non-tropical area of\nlow pressure developing along it. Along and ahead of the warm \nfront, a strong southeasterly LLJ will develop, supporting \nincreasing WAA and moisture transport into the area with a \nshield of moderate to heavy rain developing across the coastal \nCarolinas. Along and just behind the warm front, the precip will\nlikely become more banded and contain some embedded convective \nelements with very heavy rain. It's these bands that will pose \nthe greatest risk of flooding. The time period of most concern \nfor these heavy rates and flooding potential are overnight \ntonight into early Sun morning. Convective guidance suggests \nrainfall amounts as high as 4-6\"+ are possible if those bands \nmanage to reach the coast. As is often the case with convection \nnearing the coast, whether some of the moisture transport will \nbe cutoff by strong convection over the Gulf Stream is in \nquestion. If this happens, amounts would be quite a bit less for\nareas outside of the coastal waters. 10/00Z REFS LPPM continues\nto show amounts in the 3-6+\" range possible for the immediate \ncoast and OBX zones. Will continue Flood Watch for areas along \nthe coast, effective later this afternoon through early Sun \nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Possibility of convective elements reaching the\ncoast would warrant a tornado risk later this evening as the \naforementioned warm front lifts through ENC. Plenty of low level\nhelicity in place with the inc serly flow in place and strong \nveering wind profile downstairs. The greatest tor threat would \nlikely be focused near the srn/sern coast where better \ninstability advects onshore. For Sunday, flow becomes more \nunidirectional with swrly low level winds ensuing during the \nafternoon. While helicity wanes, better instability will be in \nplace due to daytime highs expected in the low 80s and TD's in \nthe 70s. Will have to watch for continued severe threat Sunday \nafternoon ahead of the cold front, esp if there are some breaks \nin the cloud cover. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with drying conditions\ndeveloping Sun night into Mon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strengthening onshore winds, building seas, and\nhigher than normal astronomical tides may support some coastal \nimpacts this weekend. There remains uncertainty on the magnitude\nof impacts, especially given the shorter-duration of the \nstronger winds, as well as what should be a weaker swell \ncompared to the recent Nor'easter. With that in mind, there will\nbe an increased risk of rip currents and large waves, at a \nminimum. High Surf Advisory remains in effect between Ocracoke \nand Oregon Inlets focused overnight into Sunday. Additionally, \nsome of the most vulnerable locations along the Outer Banks may \nsee at least some minor issues, especially with Sunday morning's\nhigh tide (7-9am). There may also be some minor coastal \nflooding around sounds and tidal waterways around the Crystal \nCoast due to the higher astronomical tides.",
